+    A mount that is normally busy but suddenly reports very low usage is
+    usually a sign that the filesystem failed to mount, and what looks like
+    the real disk is actually an empty directory on the root filesystem. To
+    catch that case, use a mapping with ``min_percent`` and/or
+    ``max_percent`` instead of a plain percentage. ``max_percent`` behaves
+    exactly like the plain percentage form above (emits when usage is
+    greater than or equal to the value). ``min_percent`` emits when usage
+    drops below the value, and the beacon adds a ``min_percent`` key to the
+    event so it can be told apart from a high-usage alert.
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /: 63%
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                min_percent: 10%
+                max_percent: 50%
+            - /mnt/backup:
+                min_percent: 5%
+
+    The example above keeps the existing high-usage alert on ``/`` exactly
+    as before, adds both a low- and high-usage alert on ``/mnt/nfs``, and
+    adds only a low-usage alert on ``/mnt/backup``.
+
     """
     whitelist = []
     config = salt.utils.beacons.remove_hidden_options(config, whitelist)
